{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_015hjtt4jZFhAZjoCC3C7tEM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_vrtx_01GBpjCQA6Gumpfz9t1areTD","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sarthakagarwal/Desktop/projects/prep/ai-engineering/answers/01-001-how-do-llms-work.md","content":"# How do LLMs work?\n\n**Category:** 01-llm-fundamentals\n**Question #:** 001\n**Source section:** §1 in interview-questions.md\n**Status:** `review`\n**Generated:** ralph-ai-engineering\n\n---\n\n## Framing\n\n### Why this question is asked\nThis is the baseline competency check for any AI engineering role. The interviewer is probing whether you have genuine architectural understanding (transformer internals, training vs inference) versus surface-level familiarity (\"it predicts the next word\"). Strong answers connect the architecture to production implications — why inference is memory-bound, why context windows matter for cost, how pre-training and RLHF shape behavior.\n\n### Trigger phrases\n- \"Explain how LLMs work at a high level.\"\n- \"Walk me through what happens when I send a prompt to GPT-4.\"\n- \"How does a language model actually generate a response?\"\n\n### What it tests\nDepth of mental model for transformer-based generative models — from training objective through autoregressive inference and alignment fine-tuning.\n\n---\n\n## Answer\n\n### Concept\nLarge Language Models are neural networks — almost universally transformer-based — trained to predict the next token in a sequence. By doing this at massive scale (billions of parameters, trillions of tokens), they learn statistical patterns that encode world knowledge, reasoning strategies, and language fluency. At inference time, they sample token-by-token from a learned probability distribution over a vocabulary (typically 32K–128K tokens).\n\n### Mechanism\n**Pre-training:** The model ingests a large corpus (Common Crawl, books, code, etc.) and learns via self-supervised next-token prediction. Each forward pass: input tokens → token embeddings + positional encodings → N transformer layers (each: multi-head self-attention + MLP + layer norm + residual) → output logits over vocabulary → cross-entropy loss. Optimization is via AdamW with a cosine learning rate schedule. Gradient checkpointing and mixed-precision (BF16/FP8) are used to fit training on GPU clusters (e.g., 512–2048 H100s for a 70B model).\n\n**Alignment (post-training):** Raw pre-trained models are unhelpful assistants. Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) on curated instruction-response pairs shapes behavior. RLHF (or DPO) then aligns outputs with human preferences — a reward model scores responses, and PPO (or direct preference optimization) updates the policy to maximize reward without drifting too far from SFT baseline.\n\n**Inference (autoregressive decoding):** Given a prompt, the model runs a forward pass to get logits for the *next* token, samples from those logits (temperature, top-p, top-k control diversity), appends the token to the sequence, and repeats. The KV cache stores key/value tensors from prior layers so they aren't recomputed each step — critical for latency. Frameworks like vLLM use PagedAttention to manage KV cache memory efficiently across many concurrent requests.\n\n### Example / Tradeoff\nGPT-4 is believed to be a Mixture-of-Experts model (~8 experts, ~2 active per token) serving inference on A100/H100 clusters via vLLM-style batching. Input tokens cost ~$0.01/1K (as of late 2024) largely because each token requires a full forward pass through hundreds of layers. Context windows of 128K tokens (Claude 3.5, GPT-4o) mean ~200 pages of text fit in one call — but quadratic attention complexity means TTFT and memory cost scale steeply. The core tradeoff: bigger context = richer reasoning, higher latency, higher cost per query.\n\n---\n\n## Verbal script\n\n**Opening (30s):**\n\"LLMs are transformer-based neural networks trained on a next-token prediction objective at enormous scale. I'll cover the training side, then how inference actually works, and then touch on the production implications because that's where the interesting engineering tradeoffs live.\"\n\n**Core explanation (2–3 min):**\n\"Starting with pre-training: the model sees sequences of text from a massive corpus — Common Crawl, books, GitHub — and learns to predict the next token. At each step, the input tokens get embedded, positional encodings are added, and then the sequence flows through N transformer blocks. Each block has multi-head self-attention — where every token attends to every other token — followed by a feed-forward MLP. Residual connections and layer norm keep gradients stable. The output is a probability distribution over the vocabulary, and we minimize cross-entropy loss across trillions of tokens. This self-supervised setup means you don't need labels — the text itself is the supervision signal.\n\nAfter pre-training, you get a model that's good at continuing text but not at following instructions. That's where alignment comes in. SFT on instruction-response pairs teaches it to be a helpful assistant. Then RLHF — or the more training-stable DPO — uses human preference data to push outputs toward what people actually want: safe, correct, and useful.\n\nAt inference time, the model generates one token at a time. You pass in the prompt, get logits for the next token, sample from those logits — with temperature controlling sharpness and top-p/top-k controlling the candidate pool — and append the token, then repeat. The KV cache is the key optimization here: it stores the attention key and value tensors from prior positions so you only compute attention for the new token at each step, not the entire sequence. Without it, inference would be O(n²) per step.\"\n\n**Tradeoff / production angle (1 min):**\n\"The big production tension is context length vs cost. A 128K-token context is powerful for long-document tasks, but TTFT scales roughly linearly with context length (prefill phase), and KV cache memory scales linearly too — at 70B params, a full 128K context can use 16–32GB of KV cache alone, limiting batch size. That's why semantic caching, prompt compression, and tiered model routing (small model for simple queries, large model for complex ones) are first-class engineering concerns, not afterthoughts.\"\n\n**Wrap-up (30s):**\n\"So in summary: LLMs are trained via next-token prediction on web-scale data, aligned via RLHF/DPO, and serve inference autoregressively with KV caching. The architectural decisions — transformer depth, context window, MoE routing — directly drive the cost-latency-quality triangle you optimize in production. Happy to go deeper on any layer — attention internals, alignment, or the inference stack.\"\n\n---\n\n## Pitfalls\n\n- **Mistake:** Saying \"it predicts the next word\" and stopping there — **Better:** Explain the full training pipeline (pre-training → SFT → RLHF/DPO) and connect it to why the model follows instructions at all, not just generates plausible continuations.\n- **Mistake:** Treating inference as a black-box \"API call\" without mentioning autoregressive decoding, KV cache, or the token-by-token generation loop — **Better:** Describe the decode loop explicitly, explain KV cache as the key latency optimization, and note that TTFT vs token throughput are two different bottlenecks (prefill vs decode).\n- **Mistake:** Skipping the production implications entirely — **Better:** Mention that context window size directly affects KV cache memory and batch capacity, which is why cost-per-query scales non-linearly with input length, and that this drives architectural decisions like model tiering and semantic caching.\n\n---\n\n## Related questions\n\n| Question | Relationship |\n|----------|--------------|\n| [Q2: How do transformers work?](01-002-how-do-transformers-work.md) | Deep dive on the architecture introduced here |\n| [Q9: What is KV cache? How does it help in LLM inference?](01-009-what-is-kv-cache-how-does-it-help-in-llm-inference.md) | Expands on the inference optimization mentioned in mechanism |\n| [Q31: How do LLMs generate text? Autoregressive decoding process.](01-031-how-do-llms-generate-text-autoregressive-decoding-process.md) | Full treatment of the decode loop summarized here |\n\n---\n\n## One-liner recall\n\n> LLMs are transformer networks trained to predict the next token at scale, aligned via SFT+RLHF, and served autoregressively with KV caching — the context window, KV memory, and batch size form the core production cost-latency triangle.\n"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1040,"cache_read_input_tokens":50403,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1040,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a40a9342-7163-4e0b-8125-a6c0013948e9","uuid":"18e6da0c-4856-4ab8-a8e0-b895a3cd8277"}
