 Introduced in \"Attention Is All You Need\" (Vaswani et al., 2017), transformers became the dominant architecture for NLP, vision, and multimodal models.\n\n### Mechanism\nA transformer is built from stacked **transformer blocks**. Each block has two core sub-layers with residual connections and layer normalization:\n\n**1. Multi-Head Self-Attention (MHSA)**\n- Input sequence (length N, hidden dim d): each position produces Q (query), K (key), V (value) vectors via learned linear projections.\n- Attention score: for each query qᵢ, compute dot products with all keys: scores = qᵢ · Kᵀ / √d_k. Scale by √d_k to prevent gradient saturation at large d_k.\n- Softmax over scores → attention weights αᵢⱼ (how much position i should attend to position j).\n- Output: weighted sum of values: oᵢ = Σⱼ αᵢⱼ · vⱼ.\n- **Multi-head:** run H parallel attention heads with different projections, each learning different relationship types. Concatenate and project back to d.\n- Complexity: O(N² · d) per layer — the quadratic bottleneck for long sequences.\n\n**2. Feed-Forward Network (FFN)**\n- Applied independently to each position: two linear layers with a non-linearity (ReLU or SwiGLU): FFN(x) = W₂ · max(0, W₁x + b₁) + b₂.\n- Width is typically 4× the model hidden size (e.g., 4096→16384 in LLaMA-3-8B).\n\n**Positional encoding:** Self-attention is permutation-invariant — it doesn't know token order. Original transformers added sinusoidal position embeddings. Modern LLMs use **RoPE** (Rotary Position Embeddings) which encode relative positions and generalize to longer sequences than seen during training.\n\n**Layer structure (decoder-only, GPT-style):**\n- Embed tokens → add positional encoding\n- → [Masked Self-Attention + FFN] × N layers (causal mask prevents attending to future tokens)\n- → Linear projection to vocab size → softmax → sample next token\n\n**Encoder-only (BERT-style):** Bidirectional attention — every token sees the full sequence. Used for classification, embeddings.\n**Encoder-decoder (T5-style):** Encoder processes input (bidirectional), decoder cross-attends to encoder outputs. Used for translation, summarization.\n\n### Example / Tradeoff\nGPT-4 uses an estimated ~96 transformer layers with a large hidden dimension and multi-head attention. The quadratic attention cost at 128K tokens is managed by **FlashAttention** — a GPU kernel that tiles the Q·Kᵀ computation in SRAM to avoid materializing the full N×N attention matrix in HBM. This alone gives 2–4× memory reduction and 3× wall-clock speedup for long contexts.\n\n**GQA (Grouped Query Attention):** Used in Llama-3 and Mistral. Instead of H query heads each with their own K/V heads, K and V are shared across groups of Q heads (e.g., 8 Q heads share 2 K/V heads). This shrinks the KV cache by 4× with minimal quality loss — critical for serving 70B+ models at scale.\n\n**The core tradeoff:** More layers and wider FFNs = better reasoning but higher latency and memory. More attention heads = richer representations but quadratic memory growth. Production engineers navigate this via quantization (FP16→INT8→INT4), model distillation, and batch size optimization.\n\n---\n\n## Verbal script\n\n**Opening (30s):**\n\"I'd cover this in three parts: the attention mechanism itself, how transformer blocks are assembled into a model, and then the production implications — because the quadratic scaling of attention is the root cause of most latency and cost challenges in LLM serving.\"\n\n**Core explanation (2–3 min):**\n\"The key innovation in transformers is self-attention. Before transformers, RNNs processed tokens sequentially — each step depended on the previous hidden state. That made parallelism impossible during training and made long-range dependencies hard to capture because gradients had to flow through many time steps.\n\nSelf-attention fixes both problems. For each token in the sequence, we compute three vectors: a query, a key, and a value — all via learned linear projections of the input embedding. The attention score between token i and token j is the dot product of query_i and key_j, scaled by √d_k to keep gradients stable at large embedding sizes. We softmax those scores to get weights, then compute a weighted sum of the value vectors. So every token can directly attend to every other token in one operation — no sequential dependency, full parallelism.\n\nMulti-head attention runs this H times in parallel with different projections. Each head learns a different kind of relationship — one head might learn syntactic dependencies, another semantic similarity. The outputs are concatenated and projected back.\n\nThe transformer block then applies a per-token feed-forward network after attention — two linear layers with a nonlinearity, usually 4× the hidden size. Residual connections and layer norm keep training stable. Stack 32–96 of these blocks and you have a production-grade LLM.\n\nFor LLMs specifically, we use a causal (masked) variant — each token can only attend to prior tokens, enabling autoregressive generation.\"\n\n**Tradeoff / production angle (1 min):**\n\"The quadratic complexity of attention — O(N²) in sequence length — is the central engineering constraint. At 128K tokens, the N×N attention matrix is enormous. FlashAttention solves this at the kernel level by computing attention in tiles that fit in SRAM, avoiding the slow HBM round-trips. That gets you the same result with 2–4× less memory and much faster wall-clock time.\n\nGQA — Grouped Query Attention — addresses the KV cache problem. The K and V tensors for each layer grow linearly with context length and must be stored across all serving requests. By sharing K/V heads across groups of Q heads, you shrink the KV cache by 4–8× with minimal accuracy loss — Llama-3 and Mistral both use this.\"\n\n**Wrap-up (30s):**\n\"So transformers replace sequential RNN processing with parallel self-attention — every token attends to every other in O(N²) operations. Stack self-attention +